course: dictionary
lesson: 8541
title: Sayings and what they mean
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A saying is a short, well-known phrase that gives advice or states something many people believe is true. «Practice makes perfect» is a saying: if you practise a lot, you get very good at something.
explain: A saying often does not mean what its words say. «Don't count your chickens before they hatch» is not about chickens. It means do not plan on something good before it has actually happened. Look for the meaning behind the words.
explain: Saying can also be a verb, the -ing form of say. In «What are you saying?», saying means speaking words.
explain: Phrases tell you a saying is coming: «as the saying goes» introduces an old saying, and «it goes without saying» means something is so obvious it does not need to be said.
fact: A saying is a short, well-known phrase, often giving advice.
fact: «Practice makes perfect» means the more you practise, the better you get.
fact: «It goes without saying» means something is so obvious it does not need to be said.
fact: Saying is the -ing form of say: say, says, said, saying.
fact: Some sayings are myths: «feed a cold, starve a fever» is an old saying, but it is not true, so check what you hear.
exercise: What does the saying «Don't count your chickens before they hatch» mean? | Count the chickens every single day | *Do not plan on something good before it has happened | Keep your chickens in a box
exercise: «That goes without saying.» What does this phrase mean? | It is very loud | It is a secret | *It is so obvious it does not need saying
exercise: Which word is the -ing form of say? | said | says | *saying | sayings
test: In «much of a muchness» is an old saying for nearly the same», what does «much of a muchness» mean? | very large | pictures of the letter M | a long journey | *nearly the same
test: Read: «What are you saying?» What is the word saying here? | singing a song | an old saying | a short phrase giving advice | *speaking words
exam: In «a dreadful stormy day is un temps de chien, "a dog's weather"», what kind of day is being described? | *A dreadful stormy day | A sunny day at the seaside | A day with lots of snow | A calm day for a picnic
